//? Title: Octave Convolution: Frequency Exchange, Exact Geometry and Explicit Fitting
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Octave layers keep some channels at half resolution and exchange information through four
paths. We check those paths, quantify arithmetic and storage, fix a final-shortcut geometry
bug and explicitly fit the complete compact Oct-ResNet-50 with tensor Adam.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.5_attention_and_modern_convnets/octconv.cpp`.

> The method

For total C channels and low fraction alpha, low width is floor(alpha*C), high width is the
remainder. The names indicate spatial resolution rather than a guarantee of strict Fourier
band limitation. At stride one the four bias-free odd-kernel paths compute:

$$Y_H=f(X_H,W_{HH})+\mathrm{up}_2(f(X_L,W_{LH})),\qquad
Y_L=f(\mathrm{avgpool}_2(X_H),W_{HL})+f(X_L,W_{LL}).$$
\alttext(The two frequencies filter within their own stream and exchange pooled or upsampled features.)

Missing low inputs/outputs omit their paths. Pooling is 2×2 VALID average pooling, and
upsampling repeats nearest neighbours. All paths use He-uniform fan-in of the FULL input
width; BN/ReLU follow independently per stream, with learned scale/shift and epsilon 1e-5.
With matched integer-exact splits at stride one, path kernels partition the ordinary kernel,
MAC ratio is 1−.75*alpha*(2−alpha) and feature-scalar ratio is 1−.75*alpha. Entry/exit splits
and channel rounding differ. Alpha zero at STRIDE ONE equals an ordinary convolution with the
same weights. At stride two the source pre-pools before filtering, so equivalence to unpooled
ordinary stride-two convolution is not claimed. Native finite differences check all four kernels.

The source's continuing-low stride-two exchange pre-pools both input streams. The HIGH-ONLY
stride-two merge needs a correction: LH uses the original low stream directly, since it already
matches pooled high resolution. Pooling that low stream and upsampling it again cannot align
an odd 7×7 map with the final high map. The corrected path preserves the final shortcut's
spatial detail, changes that source operation, and counts LH work at the true output high side
instead of floor(side/2). A controlled 14/7-to-7 fixture and a real 224-pixel forward verify it.
Parameter names/shapes/counts stay unchanged; nonzero-alpha budgets below include the correction.

The complete ResNet-50 body retains stages 3/4/6/3, making all three bottleneck filters and
projection shortcuts octave layers. It enters high-only after the stem, uses alpha in the first
three stages, then merges the main branch in the FIRST conv1 of stage four before its spatial
stride. That stage's shortcut uses the corrected direct-LH merge. Final features are high-only,
followed by global mean and a biased classifier. All five full-width splits keep 25557032
learned scalars. Count forwards use 64 pixels; cfg.image_size=224 explicitly describes nominal
TARGET MACs, not the actual count-forward image. Alpha .25 additionally executes at 224 pixels.
Alpha-zero nominal work is 4089184256 MACs; counts exclude pooling/upsampling/BN/ReLU/addition
and memory traffic, and are not latency measurements.

The compact fit keeps all sixteen bottlenecks at quarter widths, alpha .25 and original 32×32
CIFAR images. A 3×3 stride-one stem without pooling and three remaining stage downsamplings
give a 4×4×512 final map and 1484186 learned scalars. Stage stride remains in the 3×3 octave
filter. The visible loop uses 128 fit/64 validation/64 official-test images, ten batch-eight epochs (160 updates, kept short for build time),
Adam(.001), explicit kernel L2=.0001/2, padded crops/flips and paired parameter/moving-state
checkpoints selected only by validation NLL. Every path and both stream BN leaves enter the
optimizer. All numerical preprocessing, paths, losses and updates use tensors; host images
below serve only as independent oracles. This bounded run does not reproduce paper benchmarks.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/octconv_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/octconv_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/octconv_concept.tikz > build/octconv_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/octconv_concept.svg build/octconv_concept.dvi > build/octconv_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
